  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1:  Potential for moderate rain and isolated storms \ntoday and tonight. Elsewhere, expect drizzle and rain showers at \ntimes through midday Thursday.\n\nWe remain in a chaotic mid and upper level pattern through the\nday 7 forecast, with the brunt of it being we will be under \ncyclonic flow with periods of short wave energy crossing the \narea through Thursday. Surface analysis shows a strengthening \nwedging pattern over the southern and central Appalachians, and \nthis wedge will hold, allowing moist air to overtop it, \nproducing plenty of stratus, fog, drizzle, and the occasional \nrain shower through Thursday midday. The most persistent area of\nrain early this morning was over NW NC, where storm totals last\nnight ranged from 1 to 3 inches. Recent rainfall amounts were \nmuch lighter, with Boone, North Wilkesboro, and Jefferson all \nseeing less than a tenth of an inch over the last 3 hours. \n\nToday, expect scattered showers and humid but cool conditions.\nLater this afternoon and evening, a significant short wave \narrives from the Midwest, and will trigger a few thunderstorms \nover the western mountains as well as some moderate to heavier \nrainfall after about 2 pm. Considered a Flood Watch for parts of\nthe NW NC mountains as PWATs are in the 90th percentile for \nthis time of year. However, with a strong wedge in place and not\nbeing sure of the southwestern extent of it, did not have \nenough confidence to issue it. The rainfall will continue to \npush east through the overnight tonight into Thursday morning, \nwith widespread wetting rainfall.\n \nPWATs plummet by Friday morning to a third to a half an inch\nfrom over an inch Thursday afternoon as flow becomes more NNW.\nLow pressure off the Carolina coasts will deepen and begin to \nmove north Thursday, which will help the CAD regime erode. \nExpect light rain and drizzle until about midday Thursday, with \nsky cover gradually diminishing by Friday morning. \n\nCooler weather today and tomorrow will give way to dry and mild \nconditions Friday through early next week. Fall-like, close to \nnormal temperatures are expected Friday into early next week.",
